\begin_layout Title
Set 5 - Monetary Policy Design in the Basic New Keynesian Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Gali (2008), Chapter 4
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Class Notes: 4/17/13
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Basic New Keynesian
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 Monopolistic competition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 Staggered price adjustment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Is monopolistic competition a problem? 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Yes, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{e}>y_{t}^{n}$
\end_inset

.
 This gap is known as distortion.
 Production is unnecessarily small due to monopoly power.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Is there a way (i.e., a policy) to fix it?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Yes, employment subsidies can help solve the problem.
 This method is more like fiscal policy because the central banker has no
 control over taxes and subsidies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Lets assume that the optimal employment subsidy is in place, so that the
 natural level of output is efficient.
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{n}=y_{t}^{e}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Is staggered price adjustment a problem? If so, why?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(i) 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

 can deviate from 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}^{n}$
\end_inset

.
 The central bank helps reduce the fluctuations of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(ii) Staggeed price adjustment causes relative price distortion.
 To explain, the technology level of each firm is the same and firms are
 symmetric.
 The elasticity between any two goods for a consumer is constant.
 As result, households decrease their purchases of goods with relatively
 higher prices.
 Yet, this should not happen since all the firms are symmetric.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon\rightarrow0$
\end_inset

, then the household has Leontief preferences, and it wishes to purchase
 equal amounts of all goods.
 However, if price distortion is present, then the household will purchase
 less goods overall, which leads to lower utility.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Central Bank's Objective
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A second-order approximation of the utility function leads to
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
W=\frac{1}{2}E_{0}\left\{ {\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\left[(\sigma+\varphi)\tilde{y}_{t}^{2}+\frac{\epsilon}{\lambda}\pi_{t}^{2}\right]\right\} 
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 is the welfare loss function (see Rotenberg and Woodford).
 The inflation rate enters into the welfare loss function due to relative
 price distortion.
 
\begin_inset Formula $W$
\end_inset

 is the central bank's objective function, which it wishes to minimize by
 lessening the weighted average of the output gap and inflation rate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Lets assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{-1}(i)=P_{-1}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

, i.e, there is no inherited relative price distortions.
 The optimal policy requires that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\tilde{y}_{t}=y_{t}-y_{t}^{n}=0\\
\pi_{t}=0
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{t}=0$
\end_inset

 means that there is no relative price distortion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two important observations:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 Output stability (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=\bar{y}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

) is suboptimal.
 Instead, output should vary one for one with the natural level of output
 (i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}=y_{t}^{n}$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Price stability
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is an satisfactory outcome.
 This is not an assumption, but a theortical prediction.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Class Notes: 4/22/13
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Optimal Policy
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The optimal policy requires 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}_{t}=y_{t}-y_{t}^{n}=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{t}=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 The system we derived is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\tilde{y}_{t} & = & E_{t}\left\{ \tilde{y}_{t+1}\right\} -\frac{1}{\sigma}\left(i_{t}-E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} -r_{t}^{n}\right)\\
\pi_{t} & = & \beta E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} +\kappa\tilde{y}_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\series bold
Question:
\series default
 How can the optimal policy be implemented?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Method 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An exogenous interest rate rule.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall\ t:i_{t}=r_{t}^{n}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{t}^{n}$
\end_inset

 is the natural rate of interest.
 This implies that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\tilde{y}_{t} & = & E_{t}\left\{ \tilde{y}_{t+1}\right\} +\frac{1}{\sigma}E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} \\
\pi_{t} & = & \beta E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} +\kappa\tilde{y}_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}_{t}=\pi_{t}=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is a solution.
 However, the Blanchard and Kahn condition is not satisfied.
 Thus, there are a multiplicity of equilibria.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Method 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An interest rate rule with an exogenous component.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
i_{t}=r_{t}^{n}+\phi_{\pi}\pi_{t}+\phi_{y}\tilde{y}_{t}
\]

\end_inset

Substitute 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\tilde{y}_{t} & = & E_{t}\left\{ \tilde{y}_{t+1}\right\} -\frac{1}{\sigma}\left(\phi_{\pi}+\phi_{y}\tilde{y}_{t}-E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} \right)\\
\pi_{t} & = & \beta E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} +\kappa\tilde{y}_{t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}_{t}=\pi_{t}=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is a solution.
 It is a unique solution if
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\kappa(\phi_{\pi}-1)+(1-\beta)\phi_{y}>0
\]

\end_inset

The monetary authority should respond to deviations of 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{t}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

 from their target levels with sufficient strength.
 But, since 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{t}=\tilde{y}_{t}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, then the central bank ends up setting 
\begin_inset Formula $i_{t}=r_{t}^{n}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 ex post.
 It is the presence of a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

threat
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{\pi}+\phi_{y}\tilde{y}_{t}$
\end_inset

) that achieves 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{t}=\tilde{y}_{t}=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

However, there is a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

problem
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in New Keynesian models.
 In the basic New Keynesian model, the central bank does not face a meaningful
 policy trade-off between inflation stabilization and output gap stabilization.
 To elaborate, if 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{t}=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is achieved, then the New Keynesian Phillips Curve implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}_{t}=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 So, strict inflation targeting is the best policy.
 The model is too trivial because achieving the optimal policy is undemanding
 compared to what the central bank does in reality.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Ways To Go
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Intoducing cost push shock (Gali Chapter 5).
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\pi_{t}=\beta E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} +\kappa\tilde{y}_{t}+u_{t}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{t}$
\end_inset

 is cost push shock.
 This exogenous factor causes the New Keynesian Phillips Curve to fluctuate,
 which ends up affecting the inflation expectation.
 Thus, in each period, the central bank experiences a different nontrivial
 trade-off problem of choosing the combination of 
\begin_inset Formula $(\pi_{t},\tilde{y}_{t})$
\end_inset

 that best achieves stabilization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Introducing wage stickiness (Gali Chapter 6).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Zero lower bound.
 We will never see a negative interest rate.
\end_layout

